    "lens": {
      "mechanics": "When spending financed by money and credit grows faster than the amount sold, prices rise. Gold pays no interest, so higher after-inflation bond yields increase the return an investor gives up by holding it.",
      "data": "Three forces matter. First, <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/ui/data.pdf\">weekly jobless claims rose to 200,000 from a revised 191,000 on 12 Nov</a>, a softer labour reading. Second, the <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">10-year real yield rose to 2.86% on 12 Nov</a>, lifting the return available from bonds after inflation. Third, <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10YIE\">10-year inflation compensation rose to 2.24% on 12 Nov</a>. When inflation expectations rise faster than nominal yields, real yields can fall. This time real yields rose, so bond returns after expected inflation increased.",
      "unsustainable": "If federal debt keeps growing faster than revenue, servicing it absorbs money that could go to other spending. Cuts, restructuring, transfers and monetary expansion spread the adjustment differently.",
